  "text": "**Review of *The Great Gatsby***  \n*By: Jordan Baker*\n\nF. Scott Fitzgerald's *The Great Gatsby* is a timeless masterpiece that captures the essence of the American Dream during the Jazz Age. Set in the 1920s, the novel follows the mysterious millionaire Jay Gatsby, whose lavish parties and enigmatic past have captivated the neighborhood of West Egg, Long Island. Gatsby\u2019s obsession with the green light at the end of Daisy Buchanan\u2019s dock symbolizes his longing for the past and for a love that can never be recaptured.\n\nDaisy Buchanan, the beautiful and careless daughter of the wealthy Tom Buchanan, is the center of Gatsby\u2019s world. Tom, with his imposing physical presence and entitled demeanor, represents the old money elite who look down on the new rich like Gatsby. The contrast between East Egg and West Egg highlights the social divide that Gatsby is trying to bridge\u2014between old money and new money.\n\nThe tragic ending of the novel, marked by the deaths of Gatsby, Myrtle Wilson, and George Wilson, reveals the hollowness of Gatsby\u2019s dream and the moral decay of the upper class. The story, narrated by Nick Carraway, the book\u2019s semi-autobiographical protagonist, adds a layer of personal reflection and critique.\n\nWhile the characters of Gatsby, Daisy, Tom, and Nick are well-developed, the novel's greatest strength lies in its themes and symbolism. The recurring references to the green light, the eyes of Doctor T. J. Eckley, and the Valley of Ashes all contribute to the overarching message about the corruption of the American Dream.\n\n*The Great Gatsby* is not just a story about love and wealth; it is a powerful commentary on the moral and social values of the time. Fitzgerald\u2019s lyrical prose and deep understanding of human nature make this novel a classic that continues to resonate with readers today.\n\n**Proper Nouns from *The Great Gatsby* in this Review:**  \n1. The Great Gatsby  \n2. Jay Gatsby  \n3. West Egg  \n4. Daisy Buchanan  \n5. Tom Buchanan  \n6. East Egg  \n7. Nick Carraway  \n8. Myrtle Wilson  \n9. George Wilson  \n10. Doctor T. J. Eckley  \n11. The Valley of Ashes  \n12. Jordan Baker\n\n**Total Count: 12**",
